    fix: a rebuild of the local GP on two points keeps the prior of the length scales (W3-40)
    
    At each rebuild of the local GP, the empirical prior of the log length
    scales is centred between the logs of the largest and the smallest
    pairwise distance of the training set, with a sigma of half their
    difference (gaussian_process_train.py, as MATLAB's gpdefBads.m:240-251).
    On two distinct points, the two are equal and the sigma is 0, which
    gpyreg 1.3.3 refuses with ValueError, so the run stopped; MATLAB computes
    the same zero sigma. W3-24's tilted poll reached it on the thin bands of
    the geometry suite, 3 of 60 runs at their third evaluation (reverted in
    b03a320); MATLAB's coordinate poll reaches it in no run of the
    benchmark, and any run whose local GP holds two distinct points at a
    rebuild did.
    
    Distances without spread now keep the previous prior, as a rebuild on
    targets without spread keeps the previous width of the GP mean's prior
    (KD-B6-2) and the previous centre of the output scale's (W1-26). Found by
    wave 3's gate of W3-24; the PI asked for the fix after its ruling.
    
    Test: test_gaussian_process_train.py::
    test_rebuild_on_two_points_keeps_length_scale_prior[False, True]: a
    rebuild on the log's first two distinct points keeps the previous prior
    and gives finite predictions; at the parent both raise gpyreg's
    ValueError. On W3-24's code with this fix, the three runs of the thin
    bands that crashed end at errors of 1e-7, 3e-10 and 1.2e-5.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

